1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,071
Lesson 6.4.

2
00:00:02,451 --> 00:00:05,048
Complexity of thermodynamic algorithms.

3
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:09,198
What does it mean for an
analog algorithm to be faster?

4
00:00:09,578 --> 00:00:12,004
A digital computer counts operations.

5
00:00:12,385 --> 00:00:17,016
A thermodynamic device does not
perform operations: it evolves in time.

6
00:00:17,396 --> 00:00:20,956
To compare, we must fix rules:
measure time in units of the

7
00:00:20,956 --> 00:00:25,059
relaxation time and accept that the
hardware grows with the problem.

8
00:00:25,439 --> 00:00:27,983
Today we see where the
complexities in Aifer's

9
00:00:27,983 --> 00:00:30,583
table come from and what
limitations they hide.

10
00:00:31,683 --> 00:00:32,699
Three assumptions.

11
00:00:33,079 --> 00:00:36,710
First, the device has
d oscillators and up to

12
00:00:36,710 --> 00:00:39,857
d² couplings, all evolving in parallel.

13
00:00:40,237 --> 00:00:47,450
Second, the physical timescale is
τ_r = γ/‖A‖, set by the fastest mode.

14
00:00:47,830 --> 00:00:52,203
Third, the complexity counts the total
time in units of τ_r, not including

15
00:00:52,203 --> 00:00:55,985
the loading of the matrix, which
we will see in the next lesson.

16
00:00:56,365 --> 00:00:59,349
With these rules, the analysis
of analog complexity is

17
00:00:59,349 --> 00:01:02,057
subtle, and the authors
themselves warn about it.

18
00:01:03,183 --> 00:01:07,797
Each eigenmode of A relaxes at its own
rate, proportional to its eigenvalue.

19
00:01:08,177 --> 00:01:13,605
The fastest mode sets the timescale, but
the slowest decides when we are done.

20
00:01:13,985 --> 00:01:17,116
The ratio between them is
the condition number κ.

21
00:01:17,496 --> 00:01:22,672
That is why equilibration in the overdamped
regime takes on the order of κ τ_r:

22
00:01:22,672 --> 00:01:27,127
an ill-conditioned matrix is a
physical system with very slow modes.

23
00:01:28,250 --> 00:01:29,360
Inertia helps.

24
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:34,399
In the underdamped regime, with
mass and a damping tuned to the slow

25
00:01:34,399 --> 00:01:38,579
mode, relaxation scales as the
square root of κ instead of κ.

26
00:01:38,959 --> 00:01:43,210
We simulate it with the matrix
from lesson 6.1, normalized.

27
00:01:43,590 --> 00:01:48,331
With κ equal to thirty-four, reaching
an error of one thousandth takes

28
00:01:48,331 --> 00:01:52,531
two hundred seven units overdamped
and forty-nine underdamped.

29
00:01:52,911 --> 00:01:57,509
With κ ten times larger, two thousand
three hundred seventy against one hundred

30
00:01:57,509 --> 00:02:02,049
seventy-two: the first grew eleven times
and the second only three and a half.

31
00:02:02,429 --> 00:02:06,343
It is the same acceleration as the
heavy-ball method in optimization.

32
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:08,863
Now the full table.

33
00:02:09,243 --> 00:02:15,706
For linear systems, the overdamped
method scales as d κ² ε⁻²,

34
00:02:16,086 --> 00:02:24,849
and the underdamped as d κ^(3/2) ε⁻²,
against d² √κ for conjugate gradient.

35
00:02:25,229 --> 00:02:27,884
The gain is linear in
d, but it is paid for

36
00:02:27,884 --> 00:02:30,601
with worse dependence
on κ and on precision.

37
00:02:30,981 --> 00:02:34,499
In their simulations, Aifer
and colleagues find a practical

38
00:02:34,499 --> 00:02:38,017
advantage for dimensions of a
thousand or five thousand and

39
00:02:38,017 --> 00:02:41,058
modest precision, not for
high-precision solutions.

40
00:02:42,183 --> 00:02:45,104
A temptation: if we multiply b by a large

41
00:02:45,104 --> 00:02:48,595
constant, the time formula
drops with its square.

42
00:02:48,975 --> 00:02:52,105
But initializing the
system costs an energy

43
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:56,400
proportional to bᵀ A⁻¹ b, which
rises with the same square.

44
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:01,612
What stays bounded is
the product: energy times

45
00:03:01,612 --> 00:03:06,237
time is at least 2κ² d
β⁻¹ τ_r / (ε²(1 − P)).

46
00:03:06,617 --> 00:03:10,506
One can go faster by spending
more energy, but not for free: it

47
00:03:10,506 --> 00:03:13,962
is an energy–time relation
specific to these algorithms.

48
00:03:15,083 --> 00:03:19,707
Faced with any complexity claim, ask in
which units time is measured, how the

49
00:03:19,707 --> 00:03:24,211
hardware grows with the dimension, what
condition number and precision your

50
00:03:24,211 --> 00:03:27,994
real problem has, and whether
loading and readout are included.

51
00:03:29,117 --> 00:03:31,830
Common mistakes:
comparing relaxation units

52
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:34,291
with operations without a timing model.

53
00:03:34,671 --> 00:03:38,073
Forgetting that the hardware grows
as the square of the dimension.

54
00:03:38,453 --> 00:03:41,213
Ignoring the condition
and precision factors.

55
00:03:41,593 --> 00:03:44,744
And scaling the vector to speed
up without counting the energy.

56
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:49,117
In summary: analog complexity
is measured in relaxation

57
00:03:49,117 --> 00:03:51,969
times with hardware that
grows with the problem.

58
00:03:52,349 --> 00:03:56,310
The condition number appears
because slow modes take κ

59
00:03:56,310 --> 00:03:59,904
times longer, or only √κ
with well-tuned inertia.

60
00:04:00,284 --> 00:04:03,945
The gain is linear in the
dimension, in exchange for worse

61
00:04:03,945 --> 00:04:07,606
dependence on precision, and
energy times time is bounded.

62
00:04:08,717 --> 00:04:12,579
To practice: compute the
relaxation time of the slow mode.

63
00:04:12,959 --> 00:04:15,011
Find the optimal damping.

64
00:04:15,391 --> 00:04:19,924
Find the dimension beyond which the
thermodynamic method wins in the example.

65
00:04:20,304 --> 00:04:24,187
And explain why scaling the vector
does not make it infinitely fast.
